1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,440
Module six, video 6.2,

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:06,820
when something changes, correct the document that owns the fact

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:12,020
A change goes up to its source, and everything below is produced again.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:13,840
Right. So, um,

5
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:15,960
thinking about this practically,

6
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:21,200
when a service is actively in use in your ministry and someone asks for a change,

7
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:26,880
why is changing the screen or the running system where the problem was seen problematic?

8
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:31,560
Well, simply put, the quickest fix is the one that brings the problem back later.

9
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:38,480
Mm-hmm. Because, uh, if a change is made only to the running system, the underlying source remains completely unaltered.

10
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:46,020
Right. So the next time your program generates the service from that source document, the old fault simply overwrites the surface level fix.

11
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:47,920
Ugh, leaving it all fragmented.

12
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:52,520
Exactly. It leaves the architecture fragmented and, uh, very costly to change.

13
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:53,200
Okay, so

14
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:59,180
what is the SDD method's rule for every change to ensure that the old fault does not return?

15
00:01:00,140 --> 00:01:02,800
The rule requires three steps executed in order.

16
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:06,040
Okay. One, find the document that owns the fact.

17
00:01:06,160 --> 00:01:09,500
Mm-hmm. Two, correct the fact there and nowhere else.

18
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:12,680
And three, produce everything below it again.

19
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:15,180
Produce everything below it again. Yes. Right.

20
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:22,160
Yes. Because correcting a fact where it was noticed, um, leaves the owning document unchanged.

21
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:27,780
By updating only the source, we ensure that one statement of each fact is the only arrangement that stays true.

22
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:33,840
Now, when the behavior of the service itself must change, why does holding that order matter even more?

23
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:34,800
Well,

24
00:01:36,120 --> 00:01:40,900
failing to hold the order leads to services that are, uh, siloed and duplicated.

25
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:43,800
When behavior changes, there are four steps to follow in order.

26
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:45,160
Okay, what are they?

27
00:01:45,940 --> 00:01:46,160
One,

28
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:49,440
first the story of the goal agreed. Right.

29
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:51,600
Two, then the screens.

30
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:52,540
Three,

31
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:57,440
then the walkthrough. Mm-hmm. Four, only then what was built.

32
00:01:57,860 --> 00:01:58,880
Only then what was built.

33
00:01:59,320 --> 00:01:59,660
Got it.

34
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:05,360
Because, uh, a screen changed ahead of its story is a decision nobody agreed to.

35
00:02:06,660 --> 00:02:07,160
That makes sense.

36
00:02:08,259 --> 00:02:13,760
So how does this trace through an institution's request, um, maybe using Progressa?

37
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:14,700
Sure.

38
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,380
Using Progressa, and just to be clear, strictly as the fictional country of these examples.

39
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:21,220
Right, of course.

40
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:28,200
Let's say Harbourview University College asks PHEQA, and only PHEQA, to change its name.

41
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,220
So the Ministry of Education is not asked.

42
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:38,360
Correct. Now, a registration officer could, uh, write the new name into the register before your minister approves.

43
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:40,340
Right, they could just manually enter it.

44
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:41,640
They could. Mm-hmm.

45
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:47,520
But the screen does not own that fact. PHEQA's register of business rules owns it.

46
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:53,160
Ah, okay. So it is corrected there to require minister approval from MoEYS.

47
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:57,820
And finally, PHEQA's registrar, who owns the rules, confirms the correction.

48
00:02:58,120 --> 00:02:58,680
Okay, so then

49
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:03,200
what does the program list as stale, and what will the recording actually show?

50
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:06,720
Well, the program lists everything made from the old version of the rule.

51
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,600
Which includes... Uh, that includes the shared groundwork, uh, the goals, their screens, the walkthroughs- Mm-hmm... and the application itself.

52
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:20,520
But importantly, MoEYS's documents are not stale because none of them was made from PHEQA's rule.

53
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:22,080
Right. That distinction makes sense.

54
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:29,820
Exactly. Now, the recording will show the list of everything the change reaches, uh, the application generated again, and the change on the running service.

55
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:35,820
Okay. And it passes only when the list names everything the change reaches and nothing is stale afterwards.

56
00:03:36,100 --> 00:03:39,180
Mm-hmm. A change goes up to the document that owns the fact.

57
00:03:39,640 --> 00:03:41,980
Correct it there, then produce everything below it again.

58
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:43,800
Sources are in the video description.
